Advantages:
1. Real-time monitoring capability, enabling continuous observation of global sea ice coverage;
2. High-resolution imagery, capable of distinguishing sea ice conditions at resolutions up to 0.5 meters;
3. Cost-effective resource assessment, reducing the reliance on traditional field measurements;
4. Broad applicability in harsh regions, including polar areas where ground data is difficult to obtain;

Limitations:
1. Weather dependency, cloud cover may affect data accuracy;
2. Sea ice thickness detection limitations, primarily reflecting surface conditions;
3. Limited dynamic tracking capabilities, difficulty in real-time tracking movement processes;
4. Need for calibration validation, satellite data may have systematic errors.
